 ## Overview
-This method enables multi-view mini-batch training for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to reduce gradient variance and stabilize optimization, addressing the limitation that standard 3DGS rasterization forces single-view processing [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard 3DGS by allowing multiple images to contribute to a single gradient update, while overcoming the naive multi-view overhead through a thread-efficient partial rendering pipeline and adapting the Adaptive Density Control (ADC) mechanism to handle multi-plane gradients [S1, unofficial].
+This method enables stable optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by processing multiple camera views per training iteration, addressing the high variance of gradients inherent in the standard single-view approach. While standard 3DGS renders one image per iteration due to rasterization overhead, this method uses thread-efficient partial rendering to gather pixels from multiple views without idle thread waste, and introduces a 3D-space D-SSIM loss and adapted density control to handle the geometric inconsistency of multi-view gradients.
 
